<config toc toc-location=right />

Layer 1 is enscribe's semantic-HTML substrate — a vocabulary of HTML elements for academic content
(sections, figures, citations, notes, math, theorems, tables, and more). Because it is plain HTML+CSS,
a Layer 1 document is portable and interoperable rather than a bespoke format, and it is the
**canonical, archival representation** of a document — the form used for long-term storage and
interchange (JATS export among them).

Layer 1 is what the shorthand register **expands to**. Authors rarely write it by hand — not because
they cannot, but because the <a href="enscribe-shorthand.html" | Enscribe Shorthand> register is the
ergonomic way to produce it. This page introduces the idea and shows a few constructs; the
comprehensive set lives in the <a href="layer1-catalog.html" | Layer 1 catalog>.

<# #sec:expands | Shorthand expands to Layer 1 #>

Each construct below is shown three ways: the shorthand you write, the Layer 1 markup it expands to,
and the rendered result. Every example is pulled live from the vocabulary source — the same source the
<a href="layer1-catalog.html" | Layer 1 catalog> is generated from — so it cannot drift from the engine.

The Layer 1 column shows the canonical markup as the vocabulary stores it; where an element produces a
large subtree — rendered math, say — the markup elides it with `…` so the structure stays legible.

